# VoltGuard — Week 4: Hardware Deployment + Refine & Polish

## Contents

| File | Deliverable | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `deploy/deploy_pi.sh` | Hardware Deployment | Configures a Raspberry Pi as a transparent two-NIC bridge (bump-in-the-wire) and installs the IPS engine as a systemd service |
| `deploy/voltguard.service` | Hardware Deployment | systemd unit that runs the engine at boot and restarts it on failure |
| `qt/dark_theme.qss` | Refine & Polish | Dark-mode, high-contrast, large-touch-target stylesheet for a factory-floor operator display |

## Prerequisites

You need the Week 3 Rust IPS engine cross-compiled for ARM before running
the deployment script:

```bash
# On your build machine (not the Pi):
rustup target add armv7-unknown-linux-gnueabihf
cargo install cross --git https://github.com/cross-rs/cross
cross build --release --target armv7-unknown-linux-gnueabihf

# Copy the resulting binary to the Pi, into this deploy/ folder:
scp target/armv7-unknown-linux-gnueabihf/release/voltguard-ips pi@<pi-ip>:~/voltguard-week4/deploy/
```

## Deploying to the Pi

On the Pi itself, as root, from inside the `deploy/` folder:

```bash
sudo ./deploy_pi.sh
```

This will:
1. Install `bridge-utils`, `ebtables`, `iptables-persistent`
2. Bridge `eth0` + `eth1` into `br0` with **no IP address** — a real
   bump-in-the-wire is invisible at Layer 3. If you need remote
   management, add a separate management NIC/Wi-Fi interface, not `br0`.
3. Disable STP on the bridge (no negotiation delay on an industrial link)
4. Redirect Modbus/TCP (port 502) traffic into the IPS engine's intake
   port via `ebtables`/`iptables` — adjust the port/protocol if your
   target is DNP3 or a non-standard Modbus port
5. Install and start the `voltguard.service` systemd unit

## Verifying it's running

```bash
systemctl status voltguard.service
bridge link show
journalctl -u voltguard -f
```

## Applying the dark theme

In your Qt dashboard's `main()`:

```cpp
QFile styleFile(":/dark_theme.qss"); // or a direct filesystem path
styleFile.open(QFile::ReadOnly);
qApp->setStyleSheet(styleFile.readAll());
```

Widget object names the stylesheet specifically targets:
- `QLabel#statusSafe` / `QLabel#statusAlarm` — nominal/alarm banners
- `QPushButton#emergencyStop` — large red e-stop button

Set these with `setObjectName("statusAlarm")` etc. on the relevant widgets
in your dashboard code.

## Final Review checklist (per the project plan)

- [ ] Compiled, standalone desktop dashboard runs on the operator PC
- [ ] IPS engine + physics engine deployed on the Pi as a bump-in-the-wire
- [ ] Dashboard connects to the Pi's telemetry stream and reflects live
      drops/alarms
- [ ] Dark theme applied and legible under bright/fluorescent lighting